# Course-eligibility relational reasoning (real-inference demo).
#
# A genuinely different task from a single key-value lookup: the
# answer here is never a fact stated directly in the input (unlike a
# lookup, where the answer is present verbatim, key -> value). The
# input is real, natural-language sentences -- not a symbol table --
# describing (a) up to 3 courses a student has already completed, and
# (b) each of 6 candidate courses' single prerequisite -- but nowhere
# does any sentence say "the student is eligible for X". Example,
# written in plain words (the model sees only embeddings of these
# actual sentences, one word-embedding table shared across every
# sentence role):
#
#     Maya has completed CS101.
#     Maya already finished MA101.
#     CS201 requires CS101 as a prerequisite.
#     MA201 needs MA101 before you can enroll.
#     PHY101 has no prerequisite.
#     BIO101 requires nothing beforehand.
#     -> eligible: PHY101
#
# Every example draws its own random 6-course subset from a 24-course
# pool (tools/models/prepare_relational_demo.py's COURSE_POOL), its
# own random student name, and (often) an irrelevant distractor
# completed-course sentence the model must learn to ignore -- so no
# example repeats the same course-to-slot identity, and the sentence
# wording itself varies per example (several templates per fact
# kind). Nothing here is a fixed symbol table: only the *presence of
# the same course name* across two sentences is a stable signal, and
# v1 Tensora never sees a token or a symbol -- every sentence is
# turned into a float vector by a from-scratch word-embedding +
# mean-pool encoder entirely in PyTorch before Tensora runs (v1
# Tensora has no embedding/gather/tokenizer op of its own).
#
# Solving it needs two chained lookups, not one: attend over the
# completed-course facts to build a "profile" of what the student
# knows (hop 1), then, separately for each candidate course, check
# whether *that* course's prerequisite is covered by the profile, and
# whether the course itself is already done (hop 2) -- exactly the
# "attend to completed courses, attend separately to requirements,
# combine, ignore what doesn't apply" reasoning a lookup table cannot
# do. tools/models/prepare_relational_demo.py trains this once in
# PyTorch over real sentences with a 24-course pool and randomized
# phrasing; held-out accuracy lands around 55-60% (run the script to
# see the exact figure) -- clearly above the 1/6 ~ 17% chance rate,
# confirming genuine two-hop fact-chaining, but, honestly, well short
# of a clean answer, since this is a genuinely hard task once the
# input is real text instead of a symbol table. The comparison below
# is still exact (Tensora vs. this same imperfect PyTorch model), same
# as every other real-inference demo; only the *task* is harder, not
# the tolerance.
#
# Hop 1 is single-head softmax attention (Q@Kt / softmax / A@V), but
# the query is a fixed
# *learned* "profile query" (Wq1) rather than a per-example token --
# there is nothing to look up yet, just a fixed question ("what has
# this student completed?") asked of the completion facts. The
# null-padding completion slots (fewer than 3 courses completed) are
# never masked by hand -- v1 Tensora has no masking op either -- the
# attention weights simply learn to give them low score.
#
# Hop 2 has no attention op at all: `profile` is projected two ways
# (Wcov = "does this cover a course's prerequisite?", Walr = "is this
# course itself already done?") and matmul'd against every candidate
# course's prerequisite embedding (Xpp) and its own embedding (Xpc),
# giving one `coverage` and one `already` score per course. The two
# per-course score tensors are combined with two learned scalars
# (Wa, Wb) and a bias -- plain elementwise `*`/`+`, since v1 Tensora
# has no stack/concat op to feed a combining MLP instead.
#
# The trailing `sq=1` axis (hop 1's attention query has exactly one
# row -- there is only one "profile" per example) is dropped with
# `sum(..., axis=sq)`, a no-indexing "squeeze a singleton axis" trick
# (multiply/sum in place of a slicing op, which v1 Tensora has none of).
#
# Scale = 1/sqrt(dk) = 1/sqrt(32); the prep script uses the same
# constant so the reference matches.
#
# The batch axis b is 200: this task's accuracy is well under 100%, so
# a bigger-than-usual held-out batch is needed for the
# printed accuracy number to be statistically meaningful rather than
# noise from a handful of examples.
input  Xcomp: Tensor[b=200, ncomp=3, d=32]
input  Xpp:   Tensor[b=200, courses=6, d=32]
input  Xpc:   Tensor[b=200, courses=6, d=32]
weight Wk1:  Tensor[b=1, d=32, dk=32]
weight Wv1:  Tensor[b=1, d=32, dv=32]
weight Wq1:  Tensor[b=1, sq=1, dk=32]
weight Wcov: Tensor[b=1, dv=32, dc=32]
weight Walr: Tensor[b=1, dv=32, dc=32]
weight Wa:   Tensor[b=1, sq=1, courses=1]
weight Wb:   Tensor[b=1, sq=1, courses=1]
weight Bias: Tensor[b=1, sq=1, courses=1]

graph attn_relational {
    K1       = Xcomp @ Wk1
    V1       = Xcomp @ Wv1
    K1t      = transpose(K1, perm=[0, 2, 1])
    scores1  = Wq1 @ K1t
    scaled1  = scores1 * 0.17677670
    A1       = softmax(scaled1, axis=ncomp)
    profile  = A1 @ V1
    coverQ   = profile @ Wcov
    alreadyQ = profile @ Walr
    XppT     = transpose(Xpp, perm=[0, 2, 1])
    XpcT     = transpose(Xpc, perm=[0, 2, 1])
    coverage = coverQ @ XppT
    already  = alreadyQ @ XpcT
    covered  = coverage * Wa
    covdone  = already * Wb
    summed   = covered + covdone
    combined = summed + Bias
    logits   = sum(combined, axis=sq)
    return logits
}
